\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}
\title{Cycle counts and explicit symmetry witnesses}
\author{Human Compression project: a component of the F012 recovery}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We verify the positive graph data needed for Tables 1--5 of Goedgebeur and
Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular graphs},
arXiv:2401.08271v2, 25 June 2024. A graph with parameters
$(n,k,g,\lambda)$ is simple, connected and $k$-regular, has girth $g$,
and has exactly $\lambda$ cycles of length $g$ through each edge.
Our conclusions in this component are existence and the stated symmetry
properties. Minimum orders and uniqueness require separate exclusions.

\begin{proposition}\label{bfs}
Let $X$ be a finite simple connected graph, and let $g\geq3$ and
$\lambda\geq1$. For an edge $e=uv$, let $d_e$ be the distance from $u$
to $v$ in $X-e$, and let $p_e$ count shortest paths between them.
Then $X$ has girth $g$ and exactly $\lambda$ girth cycles through every
edge if and only if $d_e=g-1$ and $p_e=\lambda$ for every edge.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Deleting $e$ gives a bijection between cycles containing $e$ and simple
$u$--$v$ paths in $X-e$, with lengths differing by one. The shortest
such path has length $d_e$. If $X$ has girth $g$ and positive prescribed
cycle count at every edge, then every edge lies on a $g$-cycle and on
no shorter cycle, so $d_e=g-1$. Conversely, these distances exclude
every shorter cycle and give a $g$-cycle through each edge. At this
distance every path counted is shortest and therefore simple. The
bijection identifies $p_e$ with the required cycle count.
\end{proof}

Breadth-first search computes both quantities. Set the distance of $u$
to zero and its count to one. On first reaching a vertex $y$ from $x$,
give it distance $d(x)+1$ and the count at $x$. Every other predecessor
at distance $d(y)-1$ contributes its count. All shorter layers are
processed first. Induction on distance proves that this recurrence
counts precisely the shortest paths. In particular, when $v$ is removed
from the queue, all its shortest-path predecessors have contributed.
It is also sufficient to stop when the first vertex at distance $g-1$
is removed, since the entire preceding layer has then been processed.

The implementation saturates counts at $\lambda+1$. For nonnegative
integers, replacing every addition by its minimum with this cap gives
the minimum of the exact sum and the cap. Induction proves this for
the entire recurrence. Equality with $\lambda$ is therefore unchanged.
The interface bounds $\lambda$ by $10^9$, so the sum of two capped
counts is at most $2\cdot10^9+2$, within the unsigned 64-bit type used.
Distances, graph orders and adjacency indices have separate range checks.

\begin{proposition}\label{symmetry}
Suppose explicit permutations of the vertices preserve adjacency.
If their generated action has one orbit on vertices, the graph is
vertex-transitive. If it has one orbit on ordered adjacent pairs, the
graph is arc-transitive.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each bijective adjacency-preserving permutation is an automorphism,
so the subgroup they generate lies in the automorphism group. A
transitive subgroup makes the full group transitive. To compute its
orbit, repeatedly apply the supplied permutations to the current set.
The process terminates on a finite set. Inverses do not require an
extra assumption: the inverse of a permutation of finite order is
a nonnegative power of that permutation. Thus the computed closure is
the group orbit, on vertices or on ordered adjacent pairs as appropriate.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{The acquired data and resulting upper bounds.}
We retain all 731 graphs in the authors' two annotated graph6 files at
repository commit
\begin{center}\texttt{41022f4a3ad84e4502f8889c5b64132225411ca2}.\end{center}
This December 2024 repository edition is later than the fixed manuscript;
only the independently checked properties of its explicit graphs are used.
The degree, connectedness, girth and edge count tests succeed on all
731 graphs, covering 437889 edges. Five further positive witnesses were
retrieved from the public House of Graphs database:
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
\text{database identifier}&n&k&g&\lambda\\\hline
51010&60&5&5&2\\
51303&66&5&5&4\\
51013&24&6&4&6\\
51012&20&6&4&13\\
1266&57&6&5&15
\end{array}
\]
Each was checked both by Proposition~\ref{bfs} and by a separate direct
enumeration of simple cycles. The stored adjacency list and graph6
encoding agree exactly. The table register binds each of the 84 finite
upper-bound entries in Tables 1--4 to a verified graph of precisely
the claimed order. An infinite entry in the upper-bound column means
that no finite upper bound is reported; it is not a nonexistence theorem.

For each of the 118 parameter triples in Table 5, the retained graph
has the claimed order and an explicit symmetry certificate satisfying
Proposition~\ref{symmetry}. Candidate permutations were obtained with
\texttt{pynauty}; the certificate checker directly verifies their
bijections, adjacency preservation and orbits. It needs neither a
complete automorphism group nor a claimed automorphism-group order.
Consequently every reported minimum in that table is at least an
established upper bound within its specified symmetry class.

\paragraph{Verification and attribution.}
The numerical data, constructions and reported upper bounds belong to
their original providers. The deleted-edge breadth-first method is
already described by Goedgebeur and Jooken. Our contribution here is the
complete explicit correspondence, checked symmetry permutations and
separately replayable certificates, with the mathematical limits stated.
No priority claim is made for the elementary propositions.

The C++ verifier was compared with a direct-cycle implementation on all
164 connected regular labelled graphs of orders three through six,
with both matching and altered parameters. Together with malformed and
disconnected inputs this gave 332 controls, all agreeing. The full
731-graph check also passed an address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer build.
These controls supplement the proof and code audit. They are not a
proof that a supplied graph list is exhaustive. This component establishes
no uniqueness, no minimum order by itself, no open-conjecture solution,
and no complete Lean formalization.
